{"info":{"extensions":{"modified":{"x-logo":[{"oldValue":"https://docs.docker.com/images/logo-docker-main.png","value":"https://docs.docker.com/assets/images/logo-docker-main.png","op":"replace","from":"","path":"/url"}]}},"description":{"from":"The Engine API is an HTTP API served by Docker Engine. It is the API the\nDocker client uses to communicate with the Engine, so everything the Docker\nclient can do can be done with the API.\n\nMost of the client's commands map directly to API endpoints (e.g. `docker ps`\nis `GET /containers/json`). The notable exception is running containers,\nwhich consists of several API calls.\n\n# Errors\n\nThe API uses standard HTTP status codes to indicate the success or failure\nof the API call. The body of the response will be JSON in the following\nformat:\n\n```\n{\n  \"message\": \"page not found\"\n}\n```\n\n# Versioning\n\nThe API is usually changed in each release, so API calls are versioned to\nensure that clients don't break. To lock to a specific version of the API,\nyou prefix the URL with its version, for example, call `/v1.30/info` to use\nthe v1.30 version of the `/info` endpoint. If the API version specified in\nthe URL is not supported by the daemon, a HTTP `400 Bad Request` error message\nis returned.\n\nIf you omit the version-prefix, the current version of the API (v1.41) is used.\nFor example, calling `/info` is the same as calling `/v1.41/info`. Using the\nAPI without a version-prefix is deprecated and will be removed in a future release.\n\nEngine releases in the near future should support this version of the API,\nso your client will continue to work even if it is talking to a newer Engine.\n\nThe API uses an open schema model, which means server may add extra properties\nto responses. Likewise, the server will ignore any extra query parameters and\nrequest body properties. When you write clients, you need to ignore additional\nproperties in responses to ensure they do not break when talking to newer\ndaemons.\n\n\n# Authentication\n\nAuthentication for registries is handled client side. The client has to send\nauthentication details to various endpoints that need to communicate with\nregistries, such as `POST /images/(name)/push`. These are sent as\n`X-Registry-Auth` header as a [base64url encoded](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc4648#section-5)\n(JSON) string with the following structure:\n\n```\n{\n  \"username\": \"string\",\n  \"password\": \"string\",\n  \"email\": \"string\",\n  \"serveraddress\": \"string\"\n}\n```\n\nThe `serveraddress` is a domain/IP without a protocol. Throughout this\nstructure, double quotes are required.\n\nIf you have already got an identity token from the [`/auth` endpoint](#operation/SystemAuth),\nyou can just pass this instead of credentials:\n\n```\n{\n  \"identitytoken\": \"9cbaf023786cd7...\"\n}\n```\n","to":"The Engine API is an HTTP API served by Docker Engine. It is the API the\nDocker client uses to communicate with the Engine, so everything the Docker\nclient can do can be done with the API.\n\nMost of the client's commands map directly to API endpoints (e.g. `docker ps`\nis `GET /containers/json`). The notable exception is running containers,\nwhich consists of several API calls.\n\n# Errors\n\nThe API uses standard HTTP status codes to indicate the success or failure\nof the API call. The body of the response will be JSON in the following\nformat:\n\n```\n{\n  \"message\": \"page not found\"\n}\n```\n\n# Versioning\n\nThe API is usually changed in each release, so API calls are versioned to\nensure that clients don't break. To lock to a specific version of the API,\nyou prefix the URL with its version, for example, call `/v1.30/info` to use\nthe v1.30 version of the `/info` endpoint. If the API version specified in\nthe URL is not supported by the daemon, a HTTP `400 Bad Request` error message\nis returned.\n\nIf you omit the version-prefix, the current version of the API (v1.42) is used.\nFor example, calling `/info` is the same as calling `/v1.42/info`. Using the\nAPI without a version-prefix is deprecated and will be removed in a future release.\n\nEngine releases in the near future should support this version of the API,\nso your client will continue to work even if it is talking to a newer Engine.\n\nThe API uses an open schema model, which means server may add extra properties\nto responses. Likewise, the server will ignore any extra query parameters and\nrequest body properties. When you write clients, you need to ignore additional\nproperties in responses to ensure they do not break when talking to newer\ndaemons.\n\n\n# Authentication\n\nAuthentication for registries is handled client side. The client has to send\nauthentication details to various endpoints that need to communicate with\nregistries, such as `POST /images/(name)/push`. These are sent as\n`X-Registry-Auth` header as a [base64url encoded](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc4648#section-5)\n(JSON) string with the following structure:\n\n```\n{\n  \"username\": \"string\",\n  \"password\": \"string\",\n  \"email\": \"string\",\n  \"serveraddress\": \"string\"\n}\n```\n\nThe `serveraddress` is a domain/IP without a protocol. Throughout this\nstructure, double quotes are required.\n\nIf you have already got an identity token from the [`/auth` endpoint](#operation/SystemAuth),\nyou can just pass this instead of credentials:\n\n```\n{\n  \"identitytoken\": \"9cbaf023786cd7...\"\n}\n```\n"},"version":{"from":"1.41","to":"1.42"}},"paths":{"modified":{"/_ping":{"operations":{"modified":{"GET":{"responses":{"modified":{"200":{"headers":{"added":["swarm"],"modified":{"builder-version":{"description":{"from":"Default version of docker image builder","to":"Default version of docker image builder\n\nThe default on Linux is version \"2\" (BuildKit), but the daemon\ncan be configured to recommend version \"1\" (classic Builder).\nWindows does not yet support BuildKit for native Windows images,\nand uses \"1\" (classic builder) as a default.\n\nThis value is a recommendation as advertised by the daemon, and\nit is up to the client to choose which builder to use.\n"},"schema":{"default":{"from":null,"to":"2"}}}}}}}}},"HEAD":{"responses":{"modified":{"200":{"headers":{"added":["swarm"]}}}}}}}},"/auth":{"operations":{"modified":{"POST":{"responses":{"added":["401"]}}}}},"/build":{"operations":{"modified":{"POST":{"parameters":{"modified":{"query":{"buildargs":{"description":{"from":"JSON map of string pairs for build-time variables. Users pass these values at build-time. Docker uses the buildargs as the environment context for commands run via the `Dockerfile` RUN instruction, or for variable expansion in other `Dockerfile` instructions. This is not meant for passing secret values.\n\nFor example, the build arg `FOO=bar` would become `{\"FOO\":\"bar\"}` in JSON. This would result in the the query parameter `buildargs={\"FOO\":\"bar\"}`. Note that `{\"FOO\":\"bar\"}` should be URI component encoded.\n\n[Read more about the buildargs instruction.](https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/builder/#arg)\n","to":"JSON map of string pairs for build-time variables. Users pass these values at build-time. Docker uses the buildargs as the environment context for commands run via the `Dockerfile` RUN instruction, or for variable expansion in other `Dockerfile` instructions. This is not meant for passing secret values.\n\nFor example, the build arg `FOO=bar` would become `{\"FOO\":\"bar\"}` in JSON. This would result in the query parameter `buildargs={\"FOO\":\"bar\"}`. Note that `{\"FOO\":\"bar\"}` should be URI component encoded.\n\n[Read more about the buildargs instruction.](https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/builder/#arg)\n"}}}}}}}}},"/commit":{"operations":{"modified":{"POST":{"requestBody":{"content":{"modified":{"application/json":{"schema":{"description":{"from":"Configuration for a container that is portable between hosts","to":"Configuration for a container that is portable between hosts.\n\nWhen used as `ContainerConfig` field in an image, `ContainerConfig` is an\noptional field containing the configuration of the container that was last\ncommitted when creating the image.\n\nPrevious versions of Docker builder used this field to store build cache,\nand it is not in active use anymore.\n"},"properties":{"modified":{"ArgsEscaped":{"default":{"from":null,"to":false},"example":{"from":null,"to":false},"nullable":{"from":false,"to":true}},"Cmd":{"example":{"from":null,"to":["/bin/sh"]}},"Domainname":{"description":{"from":"The domain name to use for the container.","to":"The domain name to use for the container.\n"}},"Entrypoint":{"example":{"from":null,"to":[]}},"Env":{"example":{"from":null,"to":["PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin"]}},"ExposedPorts":{"example":{"from":null,"to":{"443/tcp":{},"80/tcp":{}}},"nullable":{"from":false,"to":true}},"Healthcheck":{"properties":{"modified":{"Interval":{"format":{"from":"","to":"int64"}},"StartPeriod":{"format":{"from":"","to":"int64"}},"Timeout":{"format":{"from":"","to":"int64"}}}}},"Hostname":{"description":{"from":"The hostname to use for the container, as a valid RFC 1123 hostname.","to":"The hostname to use for the container, as a valid RFC 1123 hostname.\n"},"example":{"from":null,"to":"439f4e91bd1d"}},"Image":{"description":{"from":"The name of the image to use when creating the container/\n","to":"The name (or reference) of the image to use when creating the container,\nor which was used when the container was created.\n"},"example":{"from":null,"to":"example-image:1.0"}},"Labels":{"example":{"from":null,"to":{"com.example.some-label":"some-value","com.example.some-other-label":"some-other-value"}}},"MacAddress":{"nullable":{"from":false,"to":true}},"NetworkDisabled":{"nullable":{"from":false,"to":true}},"OnBuild":{"example":{"from":null,"to":[]},"nullable":{"from":false,"to":true}},"Shell":{"example":{"from":null,"to":["/bin/sh","-c"]},"nullable":{"from":false,"to":true}},"Sto